During hibernation, create_image() freezes all devices, takes a memory
snapshot, then thaws them so swsusp_write() can write the image to disk.

The original SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS maps .freeze to
pm_runtime_force_suspend, which powers off the MicroSD controller. Even
with .freeze = NULL the controller stays suspended because runtime PM
already powered it down before the freeze phase.

Fix by setting .freeze = pm_runtime_force_resume which wakes the
controller and increments its runtime PM usage_count, preventing
autosuspend during swsusp_write(). .thaw = NULL is nothing since the
controller is already active. .poweroff still actually shuts down the
controller before system poweroff.

---
 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
@@ -631,9 +631,18 @@ static void dw_mci_rockchip_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 }
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops dw_mci_rockchip_dev_pm_ops = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend,
-				pm_runtime_force_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.resume = pm_runtime_force_resume,
+	/* .freeze force-resumes the controller so swsusp_write() can trigger
+	 * block I/O while it is powered, clocked, and tuned by SDR104.
+	 * pm_runtime_force_resume increments the usage_count, stopping
+	 * runtime PM from re-suspending before the image is written.
+	 * .thaw is fine to use because the controller is already active. */
+	.freeze = pm_runtime_force_resume,
+	.thaw = NULL,
+	.poweroff = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.restore = pm_runtime_force_resume,
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(dw_mci_runtime_suspend,
 			   dw_mci_runtime_resume,
 			   NULL)
 };
